th  um and uh and and there's not  necessarily a  uh an overarching um  uh mandate on behalf of any of the  of any of the who or the professional  organizations for exactly what you  should do  but just trying to give you an idea of  what's done  so for microarrays  we have run this now for uh  over six years here at emory and we've  tested  uh well over 1500 brain tumors um  uh at emory uh on our chromosomal  microarray system  uh and so we've also tested some other  some other uh uh tumors that hemato  hematological  malignancies are pretty commonly uh  tested in our laboratory  uh some some kidney neoplasms a fair  variety of other solid tumors but the  but the but the majority have been cns  tumors  so as we've been um working  on our clinical specimens generating  data to assist with clinical diagnosis  of tumors  we have basically watched the field grow  and change substantially the the amount  of knowledge that exists  for uh the interpretation of copy number  abnormalities and brain tumors  uh today is is substantially greater  than when we started  uh way back uh in 2014 so  um we continue to refine our approach  uh incorporating all these new data um  and uh and and it's really exciting  uh when we learn new things and we see  how other folks  have interpreted some of the findings  that we have have observed and  really uh really uh is is uh  um it's really a great era to practice  in from my perspective  so the interesting thing for us at emory  um has been that we actually have  been in the situation of using  microarray  really is the sole whole genome assay  that we run  on our our brain tumors in-house  so uh you know there's a variety of  reasons for this  for this situation but we basically have  not  to date implemented a large ngs panel  to cover a wide variety of genes  um implicated in in cnsd inflation we  have a small  pcr based panel that covers sort of the  the  absolutely necessary genes to to run  uh a competent diagnostic panel um for  cns neoplasia things like idh1 and idh2  the  the major the major uh uh genes that you  might want to know about brain tumors  um but we haven't had we haven't we  don't have a panel that covers a lot of  a lot of mutations that we don't we  don't also have don't have a  a fusion assay uh that uh  that that covers a wide variety of brain  tumor-specific  genes so we've really had to rely on the  microarray  um we do send for methylation profiling  um on a few cases but uh that's really  just a few  we run we run the microarrays on just  about every brain tumor we see  and we have had to basically work very  hard  to to push that data as far as we can  in order to get as much information as  we can and i  i kind of feel like sometimes i am when  i'm  writing reports for uh the microarray  workups that we do i feel like sometimes  i'm sort of leaving breadcrumbs  for my clinical colleagues and uh and of  course i'll call up  my you know the colleagues in pathology  who are signing out  some of the uh the histopathology  reports  and kind of you know you oftentimes i'll  be looking at the array data and  thinking about what could this  potentially represent a fusion a copy  number break at a certain area  or you know maybe you pick up an area  that looks very complex or may all even  chromothrypsis involving a certain  portion  of a chromosome and you start to think  well maybe  maybe there's a rearrangement in this  area that i just can't quite tell by the  array  but i can suggest potentially looking  um you know to to confirm uh a  rearrangement  using some sort of targeted uh testing  either fish or  sending for for um for fusion  testing it's it's it's been an  interesting experience and so  really i we rely on the array data in  conjunction with the histopathology i  mean it's chemistry and  and this smaller mutation panel to guide  us as to  you know what what maybe do we need to  send out for additional testing or  or you know suggest to the to uh  to the to the clinicians and our  colleagues about um  some additional abnormalities that might  be relevant based upon um  doing array testing say you know you  have an loh  involving uh one of the mismatch repair  genes and i've had situations where i'll  suggest  testing for mmr multi mismatch repair  ihc based upon an area  of elevation and by golly you know the  thing turns out to be  uh uh uh you know a potentially uh  mismatch repair deficient uh neoplasm um  so there's there's a variety of things  that you can find with array data  if you really um if you really really  pour over it  um and uh and and we still we still are  excited  um by uh by buying new things that we  can find utilizing  uh this this sort of a warhorse  technology  so uh recently in cns neoplasia  there's been some additional um uh  developments that have uh  basically uh uh been incorporated  into our diagnostic paradigm um  in sort of a what i would think of as a  wh o  plus mechanism so um  since the 2016 who there was a  clear agreement in the neuropathology  community that  uh the who only meets every few years  and uh we knew that based upon the pace  of the development and knowledge  our 2016 who was going to become  a little bit long in the tooth uh sooner  than  uh than than it might have in the past  and and we knew that our diagnostic  criteria needed to be able to change  a little bit more uh flexibly um  without without necessarily having to  wait for the next who to come out  in order for us to kind of keep up with  the with the state of knowledge  and so with that in mind there was a  group  form called the c impact now group which  is the consortium to inform  molecular and practical approaches to  cns tumor  taxonomy um and uh  and and and so it's a mouthful right  it's not the formal who so it's not  necessarily  a a formal who  publication um but it's comprised of  individuals who worked on the 2016 who  and who will be working on the upcoming  who that should be coming  relatively soon um and it this group  publishes periodic updates  to propose new classification schemes  and approaches and they  they they've been working now for about  three years and they're  they've published seven updates to date  to basically suggest  updates to the diagnostic paradigms  suggested in the 2016 who  um that can influence practice moving  forward  so with that in mind i uh put together  just a couple of case studies to  uh assist in understanding um  some of the see impact now material and  then some other  portions of newer literature um  basically just a way to highlight some  some of the ways that we've  uh that we've looked at genomic  data uh here at emory um and  and uh and used it to assist us in in  diagnosis  of some of the some of the the more  uh newly accepted tumor classifications  so got a couple of cases to go over and  we i'll i'll show you some pictures  it's going to be fine um we'll just go  over some h  e's quickly so our first case  is uh this is a  a man in his 60s it's an infiltrative  front of parietal mass  it's focally enhancing on on  mr imaging and uh so this is a  this is a uh an h e uh picture here  and it shows you basically hypercellular  brain tissue and if you  you're you have a fairly broad  differential diagnosis of  hypercellular brain tissue um but if you  look  fairly closely at these cells um  you'll notice that they are  fairly similar from one to the next  fairly similar and fairly similarly  odd looking so to a histopathologist  uh this is the picture of an  infiltrating glioma  these cells look glial and they are  spread  through the background brain in a way  that really  only glial cells or infiltrating gliomas  would do  so and when you're dealing with an  infiltrating glioma in a patient in this  age range you're pretty worried  um you're thinking about something that  could be  um aggressive um and so  so therefore your your senses are  tingling you're looking around for some  of the cardinal features  of a glioblastoma and of course the  first thing you want to be looking for  is mitotic activity  and and i've just highlighted here a  mitotic figure  that we found in this tumor but  we did not find the cardinal features  of glioblastoma from a histopathological  perspective there was no necrosis  no proliferative vasculature so we could  not make the diagnosis of gbm  based upon the um  the the biopsy material that was sent to  us  so what we did was we ran an array on  the material and  what we saw this is a whole genome view  of  the array in this case and  just to summarize first things first i'm  actually  showing you the fact that this is a  triploid tumor we  interpreted this array in the context of  a triploid  background um and you'll notice that  that  uh that the uh that the yellow line  there is sort of the  new normal that we have assigned to this  tumor  and in that context we interpreted  there being a whole chromosome gain of  seven  whole chromosome loss of 10 in this  tumor  and you can't really see it because it's  a little bit out of the frame but there  is an amplification  of each of the egfr locus on 7p  and then there's also a homozygous loss  of of 9p that includes the cdk into  a gene and so with this in mind  if we were to think about what  abnormalities we would  typically see with a classic  idh wild-type glioblastoma  well they would be many of the things  that i just mentioned you would look for  a whole chromosome k-7  you look for whole chromosome loss of 10  and you would look for an egfr  amplification those are things that we  commonly see in idh wild type  glioblastomas  so in summary basically we've got  an r tumor uh uh the  the one that we're attempting to  diagnose we see many of the  abnormalities that we often see in idh  wild type gpms but we don't have  the histopathologic findings to be able  to say that  so um the verdict here is that we're  dealing with something that's  troublesome we are  seriously worried about this tumor and  we  we really think that it's a glioblastoma  based upon the  uh the genomic data that we have  but um so  the the the then the question comes well  how how could this be  i mean there has to it has to be a gbm  look at look at the  all of the abnormalities but  how how could it be that it doesn't have  the histopathologic abnormalities well  it's a nasal needle biopsy sometimes the  surgeons miss the foci of necrosis they  miss the vascular proliferation  um and so uh you know you just can't  quite make it there because  the abnormalities just aren't present on  the slides and then the other thing is  well maybe the tumor hasn't quite yet  developed those  his diagnostic histopathologic features  remember this is just this  by imaging this was focally enhancing  and usually with something that has a  bunch of necrosis and vascular  proliferation you're going to see  ring enhancement that's the classic  imaging feature of a gpm  so in any event we're sitting there we  got a problem here we we have something  that we believe is a gbm  but we can't quite call it based upon  what we've got  on the slides so um this was a  fairly common issue in neuropathology  and uh the the uh see impact now  group got together and basically  wondered well are there is there a way  that are we do we feel confident enough  that there are certain molecular  findings  that would allow us to say this is a  glioblastoma  even without having some of the these  long-time  hallmark histopathologic features the  answer was yes  so they felt like um  uh there was there were certain  abnormalities that we could say okay if  you've got  if you're in an the cerebrum  of an adult and you have an infiltrating  astrocytic tumor um that's idh wild type  uh if you have certain abnormalities you  can basically say  it's a glioblastoma all right it's  this is important it it it it's a  diagnosis that  allows the entry of gbm into the  diagnostic line  and it allows patients to get on to  clinical trials perhaps because they  have glioblastoma  issued by the pathologist and it really  familiarizes patients in treating  physicians  with the expected behavior of the tumor  so  the concept here is of a diffuse  astrocytic glioma  idh wild type with molecular features of  glioblastoma who grade four  so you're getting you're getting the  sense here right this is another big  mouthful right  um and uh and and uh it's quite possible  that in the near future we will just  call these quote unquote molecular gbms  uh but for right now this is the this is  the diagnostic terminology that we're  using  um and this terminology was proposed  specifically  for this situation some a tumor that you  have genomic evidence that it is a  glioblastoma but you don't have the  histopathologic  findings to issue that specific  diagnosis  so um basically what were the  genomic abnormalities that that  that were proposed to satisfy the this  requirement  and uh basically it uh  it requires finding and infiltrating  glioma and the super tutorial brain of  an adult  with egfr amplification or  combined whole chromosome gain of 7 lost  a 10  or a tert promoter mutation so if you  were in that scenario  and you can find one of those three uh  abnormalities then you can use this  new diagnostic terminology  so in this case that's what we did we  called it diffuse astrocytic glioma idh  wild type with molecular features of  glioblastoma while grade 4  and i have to tell you when we first  issued this diagnosis this is  um this is not totally new it's been  around for  uh a little over a year i believe  and uh when we first utilized it our  clinical teams were thinking what are  you doing  you know this is really weird um and uh  they were somewhat alarmed by it uh but  of course we've been using  array data in our diagnostics for a  while so  we were able to kind of assuage them and  uh uh and and really since we started  utilizing this diagnosis we  used it a fair a number of times about  it once a month at least  we utilize it um and uh and now we're in  the situation where we really  very rarely utilize the diagnosis of  anaplastic  astrocytoma idh wild type because  frankly most tumors that previously fell  into that category  show the molecular hallmarks of a  glioblastoma  so this is a good another reminder that  the generation of genomic data  for cases like this is is really  critical  so case two um we're  we're dealing with a a younger gentleman  um and he has a mass in his temporal  brain  very densely cellular and you can see  even from  this low power image there are some big  cells in here that look really weird  and so on higher power indeed  this thing is jam-packed with bad cells  um and uh they are not showing  uh exactly uh all of the features of a  typical glioblastoma they don't have a  ton  of fibrillarity in the background they  are showing  clearly malignant features you can see  scattered around the slide those uh  those  very dark structures uh some of them  maybe look like little clusters of  chromosomes well those are mitotic  figures  um so there's quite a few of them here  in this image you can  uh see see one in the bottom left-hand  corner that looks a little bit like a  stick figure that's an atypical mitotic  figure  so this is another malignancy  something we're again quite worried  about  um and uh we were able to confirm that  it did  have a expression of glial markers  so it's a high-grade glioma  and we did some additional workup that  suggested that it might have an atrx  mutation we did  an immunostain for atrx and it shows you  that  there are some strong there's some  strong immune reactivity in some of the  cells the smaller cells in the  background  but our big cells are either not  showing very much reactivity at all  they're very light brown  or they're showing no immunoreactivity  they're blue  so maybe this is an indicator of an atrx  mutation  uh and then our p53 immunostain was very  strongly positive  and uh quite suggestive of a tp53  mutation  so uh usually in a younger person who  has a  a malignant glioma that has an atrx  mutation  and uh or a suggestion of an atrx  mutation and a t-53 mutation  first thing you think about is well  could it be an idh mutant tumor  and this tumor ended up not having an  idh mutation  um so then we're sitting there kind of  scratching our heads wondering what  could be going on  um and we look at our array data  and uh it shows and  a whole chromosome gain of 7 but no loss  of  of chromosome 10 um and  uh no whole chromosome loss of 10 and so  uh  you know didn't exactly fit with our the  idh  wild gbms that we just looked at  a moment ago it does have  a an amplification of 4p  that includes the pdgfra gene and then  it also has a loss of 4q  and so taking all this together we've  got a lot to think about  so this is clearly a high grade neoplasm  and a younger person we have to think  about an embryonal tumor but we're  thinking this is probably a glioma based  upon some of our  some of our findings um it  it it's idh wild type in the sense that  it doesn't have an idh mutation  right but it's not exactly the same as  the idh wild type gbms that we looked at  just previously the classic example and  the  and our uh diffuse astrocytic glioma  molecular gpm  right so um doesn't have the  some of those cardinal hallmarks that we  just talked about  however the ihc findings that  probable tp53 mutation the probable atrx  mutation and the copy number data and  particularly the  loss of 4q and the amplification of pdg  fra  raises another possibility in this tumor  and there are a variety of different  histone mutant tumors  um that have been defined uh  including uh pretty much most famously  the  2016 who defined um the  diffuse midline glioma that is  characterized by a k-27m  mutation of the h3 uh  h3 f3a gene or a variety of other  genes um histone genes and so  uh there's some of these histone  genes are seen along the midline in  children  and most commonly in adults  but we're not in the midline here so why  am i bringing this up this is  but the reason is these are not the only  cns tumors that have been associated  with  histone mutations and there are a group  of tumors called the  h3 uh g34 mutant tumors  um that in contrast to the k-27 in  mutant tumors  uh these the g-34 mutants are usually  seen  in the cerebral hemispheres and they can  show an embryonal-like histology very  densely packed tumor cells kind of  similar to what we saw  in this case they may display atrx  mutations or loss of atrx expression  they may show tp3 mutations they can  have pdgfra amplifications  and they may show a loss of 3q and or 3  3q or 4q  and so with that in mind we sent this  tumor for additional sequencing  and um the the end  diagnosis was this is an idh wild type  glioblastoma  uh who grade four however it  is a specific type uh a g34  mutant idh wild type gpm  okay so this is fundamentally a  different tumor type  than the quote-unquote idh wild-type  gbms  that we were speaking about in the first  case um  and so here the combination of  histologic and immunohis-chemical and  genomic data  sort of pointed us in the direction of  necessary  additional sequencing um  and uh and allowed us to be able to make  the most  accurate diagnosis for this patient um  and this this particular histone mutant  class the g34  mutant tumors mentioned in 2016 who but  really since that time has been uh  better established in the literature so  we were we together have reviewed the  use of copy number data in the modern  assessment of cns neoplasia  and we've summarized the importance of  some of the newer diagnostic paradigms  including  at least one of those derived from the c  c impact now project there are quite a  few others i  and i encourage you to read those papers  if you have not  already and  in summary genomic data including copy  number data  insta indispensable part of the modern  diagnosis and workup of cns neoplasia  um the exact mechanisms and the  platforms that would be utilized  is institution and somewhat situation  dependent  uh however broad spectrum mechanisms are  extremely useful in detecting unique and  recently described  entities and i  really feel like genomic information is  critical particularly broad spectrum  genomic information is critical  to my practice so  uh some acknowledgements i i really  couldn't do  uh this type of of work without  the tutelage and assistance of  the teams in molecular pathology and  oncology cytogenetics here  at emory uh i of course rely on my  colleagues in neuropathology  to assist me as well and of course  have a lot of support from my clinical  colleagues and neurosurgery  neuro-oncology  radiation oncology and uh and some  and some great informatics support from  our pathology it staff  so with that i will uh say thank you  very much  um and uh we'll take some questions and  if uh at the end of the lecture i have  not had a  chance to um to cover what you uh what  you what you're interested in please  feel free to email me thank you
 

